%U A132404 106260,60060,180180,278460,895356,3270960
%N A132404 Smallest short legs 'A' of exactly n primitive Pythagorean triangles or 0 if no such number exists.
%C A132404 Where records occur in A024359. a(12) = 13860, a(13) = 4620, and a(14) = 7140. - _T. D. Noe_, Feb 23 2012
%C A132404 From _Colin Barker_, Oct 25 2015: (Start)
%C A132404 a(11) = 872100, a(15) = 22440 and a(16) = 55440.
%C A132404 a(9), a(10), a(17), a(18), a(19) and a(20) >= 6000000. (End)
%C A132404 a(17), a(18), a(19) and a(20) >= 10^10. - _Frank A. Stevenson_, Dec 03 2025
%C A132404 From _Hriday Rungta_, Oct 17 2026: (Start)
%C A132404 Theorem (Sperner Bound and Forbidden Short-Leg Multiplicities): For any integer A >= 3 (with A odd or 4 | A) having k = omega(A) distinct prime factors, the number of primitive Pythagorean triangles with short leg A satisfies 2^(k-1) - binomial(k-1, floor((k-1)/2)) <= A024359(A) <= 2^(k-1).
%C A132404 Proof: Let M = A if A is odd and M = A/2 if 4 | A, with prime-power factorization M = q_0 * q_1 * ... * q_{k-1} where q_1..q_{k-1} >= 3 are odd. Triples with leg A correspond bijectively to coprime factorizations M = x * y (1 <= x < y). Leg A fails to be the short leg (A > B) iff 1 < y/x < 1 + sqrt(2), which is equivalent to Sum_{i in T} log(q_i) lying in an open interval of width (1/2)*log(1 + sqrt(2)) = 0.44068... Since log(q_i) >= log(3) = 1.09861... > 0.44068... for all i >= 1, no two comparable subsets T_1 subsetneq T_2 can both lie in this interval. Thus the failing factorizations form an antichain in the Boolean lattice B_{k-1}, which by Sperner's theorem (1928) has size at most binomial(k-1, floor((k-1)/2)).
%C A132404 Corollary: Since 2^k - binomial(k, floor(k/2)) > 2^(k-1) for all k >= 4, no integer A can ever be the short leg of n primitive Pythagorean triangles when n lies in any of the disjoint Sperner gaps: n = 9 (gap between k=4 and k=5), n = 17..21 (gap between k=5 and k=6), and n = 33..43 (gap between k=6 and k=7). Thus a(9) = 0 and a(17) = a(18) = a(19) = a(20) = a(21) = 0.
%C A132404 (End)
%e A132404 The solutions for the first 7 are
%e A132404 1, (3,4,5)
%e A132404 2, (20,21,29), (20,99,101)
%e A132404 3, (60,91,109), (60,221,229), (60,899,901)
%e A132404 4, (204,253,325), (204,1147,1165), (204,2597,2605), (204,10403,10405)
%e A132404 5, (5040,78319,78481), (5040,99161,99289), (5040,129551,129649), (5040,253991,254041), (5040,6350399,6350401)
%e A132404 6, (420,851,949), (420,1189,1261), (420,1739,1789), (420,4891,4909), (420,11021,11029), (420,44099,44101)
%e A132404 7, (660,779,1021), (660,989,1189), (660,2989,3061), (660,4331,4381), (660,12091,12109), (660,27221,27229), (660,108899,108901)
%t A132404 PyphagoreanAs[a_] := (q={}; k=0; If[a>=8,r=4,r=1]; Do[y=(a^2+b^2)^0.5; c=IntegerPart[y]; If[c==y, p=0; If[GCD[a,b,c]==1, AppendTo[q,a.b.c]; k++ ]], {b,a+1,a^2/r}]; PrependTo[q,k]; q); lst={}; x=0; Do[w=PyphagoreanAs[n][[1]]; If[w>x, Print[Date[],"A=",n,",w=",w]; AppendTo[lst,n]; x=w], {n,1000}]; lst
%t A132404 (* Alternative: *)
%t A132404 solns[a_] := Module[{b, c, soln}, soln = Reduce[a^2 + b^2 == c^2 && a < b && c > 0 && GCD[a, b, c] == 1, {b, c}, Integers]; If[soln === False, 0, If[soln[[1, 1]] === b, 1, Length[soln]]]]; nn = 20; t = Table[0, {nn}]; Do[s = solns[n]; If[s > nn, Print[{s, n}], If[t[[s]] == 0, t[[s]] = n; Print[{s, n}]]], {n, 5040}]; t (* _T. D. Noe_, Feb 23 2012 *)
